---
import PageShell from '../../../layouts/PageShell.astro';
import StoryArticle from '../../../components/StoryArticle.astro';
import { GITHUB_REPO_URL } from '../../../lib/github-stars';

const title = 'Why KI einfach verstehen exists — KI einfach verstehen';
const description =
	"The story behind KI einfach verstehen: why a simple question about how AI actually works turned into this project.";
---

<PageShell lang="en" title={title} description={description} otherLangHref="/de/warum/" themenHref="/en/#themenbereiche">
	<StoryArticle lang="en" eyebrow="About the project" title="Why KI einfach verstehen exists">
		<p>
			You type a question, and a few seconds later there is an answer that sounds as if someone had thought it
			through carefully. A recipe, a cover letter, a piece of code. Handy. And a little bit eerie when you have no
			idea what is actually going on.
		</p>
		<p>
			That is exactly how this project started: with a pretty simple question. <strong>How does all of this
			really work?</strong>
		</p>

		<section class="story-section">
			<h2>Using is not understanding</h2>
			<p>
				Like many others, we started out by simply using AI. For writing, for programming, for research, to
				automate things. A lot of it works surprisingly well, even without knowing what runs in the background. At
				some point, that was no longer enough for us.
			</p>
			<p>
				What happens inside a language model between question and answer? What are tokens, what are parameters?
				How does a model learn anything at all? And why can the same AI write a decent essay but miscount the
				letters in a single word?
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="story-section">
			<h2>Too shallow or too deep</h2>
			<p>
				So we started looking. There is more than enough information about AI out there. A good way in was still
				surprisingly hard to find.
			</p>
			<p>
				Many explanations are like a paddling pool: you are standing in it, but you hardly get wet. “The AI
				learned from data”, done. Others are like jumping off the ten-metre board: two paragraphs in, the
				formulas arrive, and anyone who could not already swim goes under. What we missed was the pool in
				between. Shallow enough to get in, and deep enough that in the end you understand <strong>why</strong>
				something works the way it does.
			</p>
			<figure class="story-figure">
				<img
					src="/warum/becken.png"
					alt="Three pools side by side: on the left a paddling pool with a bored child standing in it, on the right a diving tower above deep water with a hesitant child on top, in the middle a large pool with wide steps that a child happily walks down into the water."
					width="800"
					height="658"
					loading="lazy"
				/>
				<figcaption>Too shallow, too deep, or step by step: here, you take the stairs in the middle.</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<p class="key">
				<strong>That is how KI einfach verstehen came about: the thing we had been missing ourselves.</strong>
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="story-section">
			<h2>What you will find here</h2>
			<p>
				You do not need a computer science degree. It starts with the basics and goes deeper step by step, from a
				simple spam filter all the way into a language model.
			</p>
			<p>
				The project is never finished. Every answer raises new questions, and those are added bit by bit. You can
				help: the website is developed in the open as an open-source project. If you spot a mistake, an
				explanation does not click or a topic is missing, you can report it there or contribute a correction or
				addition yourself:
				<a href={GITHUB_REPO_URL}>{GITHUB_REPO_URL.replace('https://', '')}</a>.
			</p>
			<p>
				The first step is smaller than you think:
				<a href="/en/lessons/program-algorithm-model/">How does a spam filter know what junk mail is?</a>
			</p>
		</section>
	</StoryArticle>
</PageShell>
